  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out the probability that at least one of the six Millennium Prize Problems (excluding Poincar\u00e9) will be solved by specific deadlines, with AI contributing a key part of the solution. The forecast starts from January 20, 2025. The deadlines are 2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, and 2050, and the probabilities need to be non-decreasing over time.\n\nFirst, I need to understand each of the six problems and the likelihood of AI contributing to their solutions. The problems are:\n\n1. Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer conjecture\n2. Hodge conjecture\n3. Navier-Stokes existence and smoothness\n4. P vs NP\n5. Riemann hypothesis\n6. Yang-Mills existence and mass gap\n\nFrom what I know, these are all notoriously difficult problems that have resisted solution for decades. Each is in a different area of mathematics, so maybe AI's impact could vary across them.\n\nI should consider how AI can contribute to solving these. AI can assist in pattern recognition, theorem proving, and large-scale computations. Maybe it's most applicable to problems with a computational or combinatorial aspect, like P vs NP or Navier-Stokes. But pure number theory problems might be harder for AI to tackle.\n\nLooking at the probability timeline:\n\n- 2026: Very close, so the probability is likely low. Maybe 0.01, under 1%.\n- 2030: Four more years. AI will have advanced, but these problems are tough. Maybe 0.05, 5% chance.\n- 2035: Five more years from 2030. By then, AI might have more established tools for mathematics. Maybe a 10% chance, 0.10.\n- 2040: Ten years after 2030. By then, if no breakthroughs, it's less likely. But considering the accelerating improvements in AI, maybe a higher chance. Perhaps 0.30, 30%.\n- 2050: Another ten years, so maybe AI becomes really advanced. Probability could be around 0.50, 50%, but also considering that maybe some problems are just too hard, it might be a bit lower, say 0.50.\n\nI should make sure each probability is non-decreasing. So 2026: 0.01, 2030: 0.05, 2035: 0.10, 2040: 0.30, 2050: 0.45. Maybe 0.45 instead of 0.50 for 2050 to account for the possibility it might never happen. Or maybe it's better to have 0.50 as that's a common threshold. Alternatively, perhaps 0.40 to be cautious.\n\nWait, but I should think about whether all problems are equally likely. Maybe some are easier for AI. For example, maybe P vs NP has more computational approaches where AI could help. Or perhaps Yang-Mills could involve complex simulations that AI could assist with.\n\nBut given that all are open, each is extremely difficult. So even by 2050, it's still uncertain. Maybe a 50% chance is too high, so perhaps lower.\n\nAlternatively, the probabilities could be:\n\n- 2026: 0.01\n- 2030: 0.03\n- 2035: 0.08\n- 2040: 0.20\n- 2050: 0.35\n\nBut the user's example had 0.45 for 2050, which seems high. Maybe the increase should be more gradual.\n\nWait, but in the initial example, the probabilities went from 0.01 to 0.10 to 0.25 to 0.50 to 0.80, which seems too optimistic. I think the actual likelihood is lower, especially considering these are millennium problems.\n\nPerhaps adjusting the probabilities to be more conservative:\n\n2026: 0.01\n\n2030: 0.05\n\n2035: 0.10\n\n2040: 0.20\n\n2050: 0.30\n\nThis way, it's a steady increase, but still not too high, considering the difficulty.\n\nAlternatively, considering that AI could make more progress in theorem proving with hybrid approaches, maybe scaling higher.\n\nBut overall, given the time frames, I think the probabilities should increase, but not too rapidly. Let me settle on:\n\n2026: 0.01 (1%)\n\n2030: 0.05 (5%)\n\n2035: 0.10 (10%)\n\n2040: 0.20 (20%)\n\n2050: 0.30 (30%)\n\nThis seems reasonable, reflecting increasing confidence over time as AI improves, but not overestimating given the problems' difficulty.\n</think>\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.01,\n  \"2030\": 0.05,\n  \"2035\": 0.10,\n  \"2040\": 0.20,\n  \"2050\": 0.30\n}",
